window.MM['radio_golden_2025_feb_morning']=[
/*1*/ {"q":"Metformin side effect:","o":["Diarrhea and gas","Increased metabolism by cytochrome P450","Weight gain","Cannot be used with sulphonylurea and insulin","Hepatic toxicity"],"a":0,"e":"Metformin acts on intestinal flora and bile acid metabolism, causing diarrhoea and flatulence in up to 30% of patients. It does not induce P450 enzymes and is weight-neutral.<br><br>Take Notes: Sulphonylureas → weight gain | Metformin → GI upset (diarrhoea + gas) | Metformin = combinable with insulin and sulphonylureas."},
/*2*/ {"q":"After appendectomy, wound healing is by:","o":["Primary intention","Secondary intention","Tertiary intention","Delayed healing","Granulation healing"],"a":0,"e":"A clean surgical incision with apposed edges and minimal tissue loss heals by primary intention with rapid epithelialisation and minimal scarring.<br><br>Take Notes: Secondary intention = open wounds with granulation | Tertiary = delayed closure | Primary intention = clean apposed surgical wound."},
/*3*/ {"q":"Investigations for Hashimoto's thyroiditis include:","o":["Anti-thyroglobulin and anti-TPO","Anti T3","TFTs","Anti-thyroglobin alone","Thyroid scan"],"a":0,"e":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is autoimmune; anti-microsomal (anti-TPO) and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies destroy thyroid parenchyma and are diagnostic markers.<br><br>Take Notes: Graves → TSI/TRAb | TFTs → show dysfunction not cause | Hashimoto = anti-TPO + anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (FA + Pathoma)."},
/*4*/ {"q":"Female with burning urination, UTI, culture shows oxidase positive non-lactose fermenting greenish colonies with fruity smell. Drug of choice:","o":["Ciprofloxacin","Ceftazidime","Ceftriaxone","Azithromycin","Nitrofurantoin"],"a":1,"e":"Greenish (pyocyanin) oxidase-positive, non-lactose fermenting colonies with fruity smell are Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Ceftazidime is the preferred anti-pseudomonal cephalosporin.<br><br>Take Notes: Ciprofloxacin = alternative | Ceftriaxone = no Pseudomonas cover | Pseudomonas = oxidase+, green, fruity = ceftazidime."},
/*5*/ {"q":"Patient with meningitis. Which function of CSF was disrupted?","o":["Absorption of CSF","Less nutrition to brain tissue","Regulation of ICP","Protection of brain tissue by CSF","Excretion of CSF"],"a":0,"e":"Inflammatory exudate in meningitis blocks the arachnoid villi, impairing CSF absorption and causing communicating hydrocephalus and raised ICP.<br><br>Take Notes: Protection is mechanical (intact) | Nutrition is minor role | Meningitis = arachnoid villi blocked = CSF absorption disrupted."},
/*6*/ {"q":"Campylobacter jejuni drug of choice in bloody diarrhoea leading to enterocolitis:","o":["Azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Penicillin","Ceftriaxone","Metronidazole"],"a":0,"e":"Campylobacter jejuni causes invasive bloody diarrhoea; azithromycin is first-line due to widespread fluoroquinolone resistance and short treatment duration.<br><br>Take Notes: Ciprofloxacin → high resistance | Ceftriaxone weak vs Campylobacter | Campylobacter bloody diarrhoea = azithromycin."},
/*7*/ {"q":"A 2-year-old child presents with metabolic acidosis (pH 7.2, HCO3- 14) and hypokalemia (T 2.6). Sodium is normal. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Renal tubular acidosis (RTA)","Lactic acidosis","Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","Dehydration","Salicylate toxicity"],"a":0,"e":"Normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, normal sodium, and absence of ketones/lactate points to renal tubular acidosis from impaired H+ secretion or HCO3 reabsorption.<br><br>Take Notes: DKA → hyperkalemia + ketones + high glucose | Lactic acidosis = high anion gap | RTA = normal-gap acidosis + hypokalemia."},
/*8*/ {"q":"What is the type of mutation in Li-Fraumeni syndrome?","o":["Rb gene","MEN-1","TP53 inhibiting apoptotic function","K-RAS","BRCA1"],"a":2,"e":"Li-Fraumeni is an autosomal dominant cancer predisposition from germline TP53 mutation; loss of p53 tumour suppressor function impairs apoptosis and DNA-damage response.<br><br>Take Notes: Rb → retinoblastoma | MEN-1 → endocrine tumours | Li-Fraumeni = germline TP53 inactivation = failed apoptosis."},
/*9*/ {"q":"In a patient with steatohepatitis, which histological finding in liver cells is most characteristic?","o":["Bilirubin deposits","Fatty acid accumulation","Glycogen inclusions","Protein aggregates","Iron deposits"],"a":1,"e":"Steatohepatitis (alcoholic or non-alcoholic) shows hepatocyte ballooning with macrovesicular fatty acid and triglyceride accumulation, along with Mallory-Denk bodies and lobular inflammation in the liver lobules.<br><br>Take Notes: Bilirubin deposits = cholestasis | Iron deposits = haemochromatosis | Steatohepatitis = macrovesicular fatty acid accumulation in hepatocytes."},
/*10*/ {"q":"Elderly patient with fever, malaise, lower abdominal pain, O/E tenderness in left iliac fossa, history of loose stools with blood. Most appropriate investigation:","o":["X-ray","Colonoscopy","Sigmoidoscopy","CT abdomen","Barium enema"],"a":1,"e":"Left iliac fossa tenderness with bloody diarrhoea in an elderly patient suggests diverticulitis or colitis; colonoscopy visualises the whole colon, biopsies lesions, and excludes malignancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Sigmoidoscopy → only distal colon | Barium enema → limited diagnostic value | Bloody diarrhoea + LIF pain = colonoscopy."},
/*11*/ {"q":"During inguinal hernia repair surgery, which nerve is most at risk of injury?","o":["Ilioinguinal, iliohypogastric, and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve and genitofemoral nerve","Obturator nerve and lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Femoral nerve and pudendal nerve","Sciatic nerve and posterior femoral cutaneous nerve"],"a":0,"e":"The ilioinguinal nerve traverses the inguinal canal; iliohypogastric and genital branch of genitofemoral are nearby. All three are at risk during open inguinal hernia repair.<br><br>Take Notes: Obturator → in obturator canal | Femoral → lateral to canal | Inguinal hernia = ilioinguinal + iliohypogastric + genital branch."},
/*12*/ {"q":"Which neural tract conveys proprioceptive information from the upper limb to the cerebellum?","o":["Cuneocerebellar tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract","Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract"],"a":0,"e":"The cuneocerebellar tract is the upper-limb equivalent of the dorsal spinocerebellar tract; it carries unconscious proprioception from above T6 via the accessory cuneate nucleus.<br><br>Take Notes: Dorsal spinocerebellar → lower limb proprioception | Spinothalamic → pain/temperature | Cuneocerebellar = upper limb to cerebellum."},
/*13*/ {"q":"Most consistent investigation for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN):","o":["ASO titer","Blood culture","Complement levels (C3, C4)","Urine culture","ANA"],"a":2,"e":"PSGN is mediated by immune complexes that consume complement; low C3 (with normal C4) is the most consistent finding, normalising in 6-8 weeks.<br><br>Take Notes: ASO → confirms recent strep but not GN activity | Blood/urine culture → negative | PSGN = low C3 = most consistent."},
/*14*/ {"q":"Patient with swelling on anterior aspect of arm with tingling in lateral digits but sensation over thenar eminence intact. Superficial incision targets which nerve?","o":["Ulnar nerve","Superficial branch of the median nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Lateral-digit tingling with intact thenar sensation (palmar cutaneous branch spared) indicates compression of the superficial sensory branch of the median nerve in the forearm.<br><br>Take Notes: Median nerve proper → would affect thenar too | Palmar cutaneous branch → arises before carpal tunnel | Superficial median = lateral digits without thenar."},
/*15*/ {"q":"Which DPP-4 inhibitor does not require dose modification and is primarily excreted in the stool?","o":["Linagliptin","Vildagliptin","Teneligliptin","Saxagliptin","Sitagliptin"],"a":0,"e":"Linagliptin is the only DPP-4 inhibitor with primarily biliary/faecal excretion (>80%), so it needs no dose adjustment in renal impairment, unlike others which are renally excreted.<br><br>Take Notes: Sitagliptin/saxagliptin → renal clearance, need dose adjustment | Linagliptin = biliary excretion = no renal dose adjustment."},
/*16*/ {"q":"TB spine, swelling above and below same side inguinal ligament. Which muscle fascia is involved in tracking pus?","o":["Gluteus maximus","Psoas major","Sartorius","Adductor magnus","Adductor longus"],"a":1,"e":"Tuberculous (cold) abscess of the lumbar spine tracks within the psoas sheath, presenting as a swelling above and below the inguinal ligament (continuous psoas abscess).<br><br>Take Notes: Sartorius → originates ASIS, no fascial sheath | Gluteus → posterior compartment | TB spine pus = psoas sheath tracking = inguinal swelling."},
/*17*/ {"q":"A child collapsed within minutes after receiving an injection of ferrous sulphate. The most likely mediator in illness:","o":["IgA","IgD","IgE","IgG","IgM"],"a":2,"e":"Collapse within minutes after parenteral exposure indicates type I (anaphylactic) hypersensitivity mediated by IgE crosslinking on mast cells releasing histamine and tryptase.<br><br>Take Notes: IgG → type II/III delayed | IgM → primary response | Anaphylaxis = IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation."},
/*18*/ {"q":"Female with 3rd degree UV prolapse from damage to uterosacral ligament. Which structure attaches the uterosacral ligament to the cervix?","o":["Anterior pelvic wall","Rectum","Urinary bladder","Round ligament","Posterior pelvic wall (sacrum)"],"a":4,"e":"The uterosacral ligaments are the chief posterior support of the cervix and upper vagina; they extend from the posterolateral cervix to the sacrum (S2-S4).<br><br>Take Notes: Round ligament → cervix to labia | Anterior pelvic wall → pubocervical ligament | Uterosacral = cervix to sacrum (posterior pelvic wall)."},
/*19*/ {"q":"Which antiarrhythmic drug exerts its effects on both the SA and AV nodes of the heart?","o":["Propranolol","Sotalol","Amiodarone","Atenolol","Lidocaine"],"a":2,"e":"Amiodarone is a class III antiarrhythmic with class I, II and IV properties; it blocks K+, Na+, Ca2+ channels and beta receptors, acting on both SA and AV nodes.<br><br>Take Notes: Pure beta-blockers (propranolol, atenolol) → mainly SA | Lidocaine → ventricular only | Amiodarone = broad-spectrum SA + AV action."},
/*20*/ {"q":"Patient with history of MI one month ago undergoes evaluation. Which describes the predominant histological finding at this stage of healing?","o":["Fibrous scar formation","Myocardial aneurysm","Granulation tissue with inflammatory cells","Coagulative necrosis","Liquefactive necrosis"],"a":0,"e":"At 1 month post-MI, healing has progressed to mature fibrous scar formation. Granulation tissue dominates at days 7-21, and coagulative necrosis is early (days 1-3).<br><br>Take Notes: Coagulative necrosis → first 24-72h | Granulation tissue → 1-3 weeks | 1 month post-MI = mature fibrous scar."},
/*21*/ {"q":"A left-handed person presents with inability to write, understand words, and difficulty identifying the left side. Which brain region is most likely affected?","o":["Left frontal lobe","Right parietal lobe","Left temporal lobe","Right temporal lobe","Left parietal lobe"],"a":4,"e":"In a left-handed person, language is often left-hemisphere dominant; agraphia, alexia, and left-sided neglect/identification deficits localise to the left parietal lobe (dominant parietal cortex).<br><br>Take Notes: Frontal lobe → motor/Broca | Right parietal → contralateral neglect | Left parietal (dominant) = Gerstmann-type syndrome with agraphia."},
/*22*/ {"q":"A patient presents with muscle pain and difficulty standing. Which medication is most likely responsible?","o":["Corticosteroids","Colchicine","Beta blockers","Atorvastatin","Furosemide"],"a":3,"e":"Statins (atorvastatin) cause myopathy ranging from myalgia to rhabdomyolysis, presenting with muscle pain, weakness, and difficulty rising. Risk is dose-related and increased with fibrates.<br><br>Take Notes: Corticosteroids → proximal myopathy but typically painless | Colchicine → also causes myopathy but less common | Statins = classic painful myopathy."},
/*23*/ {"q":"A patient had jejunal resection 4 months ago, now presents with weakness, low haemoglobin, MCV >100. Most likely deficiency?","o":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Anaemia of chronic disease","Sideroblastic anaemia","Iron deficiency"],"a":1,"e":"Jejunal resection impairs folate absorption (proximal small bowel), causing megaloblastic anaemia with macrocytosis (MCV >100) within months. B12 stores last years and ileum-absorbed.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 deficiency → ileal resection, slower onset | Iron → microcytic | Folate = jejunal absorption = rapid macrocytic anaemia."},
/*24*/ {"q":"During mandibular surgery, how can the marginal mandibular branch of facial nerve be preserved?","o":["By tracing the nerve as it enters the gland","By identifying the posterior border of the mandible","By avoiding dissection near the submandibular gland","By using intraoperative nerve monitoring","Nerve stimulation"],"a":0,"e":"The marginal mandibular branch runs superficial to the facial vessels at the lower mandible border; tracing it as it enters the gland (or making incisions 2 fingerbreadths below) avoids injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Hayes Martin manoeuvre → ligate facial vein high | Submandibular dissection at risk | Trace nerve entry = safest preservation."},
/*25*/ {"q":"Patient undergoes barium swallow. Feathery appearance in abdomen is a feature of which structure?","o":["Jejunum","Ileum","Stomach","Gall bladder","Duodenum"],"a":0,"e":"The jejunum shows a characteristic feathery (plumose) mucosal appearance on barium studies due to its tall, closely-packed plicae circulares (valvulae conniventes), distinguishing it from ileum.<br><br>Take Notes: Ileum → smoother mucosa, fewer folds | Stomach → rugae | Jejunum = feathery appearance from prominent valvulae conniventes."},
/*26*/ {"q":"A cirrhotic patient with portal hypertension. Which part is involved at the level of the branching pattern inside the liver extending upon the peripheral capsule?","o":["Extra hepatic biliary tree","Intrahepatic biliary tree","Portal vein tributaries","Hepatic veins","Hepatic artery"],"a":1,"e":"In cirrhosis, fibrosis distorts intrahepatic biliary tree branching, producing the characteristic radiating peripheral pattern seen on cholangiography that extends to the liver capsule.<br><br>Take Notes: Portal vein → central distribution | Hepatic veins → drain to IVC | Cirrhosis branching pattern = intrahepatic biliary tree."},
/*27*/ {"q":"Garlic consumption inhibits which enzyme involved in cholesterol synthesis?","o":["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","HMG-CoA reductase","Lipoprotein lipase","HMG-CoA synthase","HMG-CoA lyase"],"a":1,"e":"Garlic compounds (allicin) inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis, mimicking statin action and reducing serum cholesterol levels.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA carboxylase → fatty acid synthesis | HMG-CoA lyase → ketogenesis | Cholesterol synthesis rate-limiting step = HMG-CoA reductase."},
/*28*/ {"q":"After starting a statin, which parameter should be measured within a few days to assess initial response?","o":["Raised LDL, cholesterol","Raised Chylomicrons","Dry cough, sometimes accompanied by wheezing","Increase in plasma levels of bradykinin","Decreased LDL cholesterol"],"a":4,"e":"Statins lower LDL cholesterol within days of starting via HMG-CoA reductase inhibition; LDL reduction is the primary monitoring parameter and target for therapy.<br><br>Take Notes: ACE inhibitor effects → dry cough, bradykinin | Chylomicrons → diet-related | Statin response = decreased LDL cholesterol within days."},
/*29*/ {"q":"A person picks different flowers and perceives various smells. Which process is responsible for distinguishing these different scents?","o":["Modulation","Integration","Adaptation","Discrimination","Transduction"],"a":3,"e":"Sensory discrimination is the ability to distinguish between different stimulus qualities (e.g., different odours); olfactory discrimination relies on diverse receptor specificities and cortical processing.<br><br>Take Notes: Modulation → adjusts signal strength | Adaptation → decreased response | Discrimination = distinguishing different stimuli (different smells)."},
/*30*/ {"q":"Patient has bronchiolar inflammation and increased productive cough. What is the progenitor cell of the structure damaged in this condition?","o":["Basal cell","Granular cell","Clara cell","Goblet cell","Type II pneumocyte"],"a":2,"e":"Bronchioles lack goblet cells; Clara (club) cells are the progenitor/stem cell of the bronchiolar epithelium and the resident cell type damaged in bronchiolitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Goblet cells → in larger airways only | Basal cells → trachea/bronchi | Bronchioles = Clara (club) cells = progenitor and resident cell."},
/*31*/ {"q":"A person steps on a rusted nail. What is the initial process in wound healing?","o":["Acute inflammatory cell infiltration","Proliferation of myofibroblasts","Collagen deposition","Granulation tissue formation","Re-epithelialisation"],"a":0,"e":"Wound healing begins with acute inflammation (0-3 days): neutrophil infiltration, vasodilation, and phagocytosis of bacteria/debris precede proliferation and remodelling phases.<br><br>Take Notes: Myofibroblasts → proliferative phase day 3-14 | Collagen deposition → weeks | Initial step = acute inflammatory cell infiltration."},
/*32*/ {"q":"A military patient came with complaint of cough not improving. He was referred to a pulmonologist who ordered a spirometry that revealed decreased FEV1, decreased FRC. Most probable diagnosis:","o":["Asthma","Pulmonary fibrosis","Emphysema","Pneumonia","Bronchitis"],"a":1,"e":"Decreased FEV1 with decreased FRC indicates a restrictive pattern; pulmonary fibrosis reduces lung volumes including FRC, unlike obstructive diseases (asthma, emphysema, bronchitis) which increase FRC.<br><br>Take Notes: Asthma/emphysema/bronchitis → obstructive, increased FRC | Pulmonary fibrosis = restrictive = decreased FEV1 + decreased FRC."},
/*33*/ {"q":"Captopril is a commonly used antihypertensive drug. A frequently observed side effect with prolonged use is a persistent dry cough, sometimes accompanied by wheezing. What is the most likely cause of this cough?","o":["Increase in plasma levels of bradykinin","Idiosyncratic reaction to the drug","Decrease in blood pressure","Decrease in angiotensin levels in the plasma","Increased histamine release"],"a":0,"e":"ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown by ACE; accumulated bradykinin in airways causes the characteristic dry cough (5-20% of patients), often months into therapy.<br><br>Take Notes: ARBs do not affect bradykinin | Low BP/angiotensin → not cough cause | ACE-I cough = bradykinin accumulation."},
/*34*/ {"q":"What is the most important determinant of prognosis in Tetralogy of Fallot?","o":["Overriding aorta","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Pulmonary infundibular stenosis","Degree of cyanosis"],"a":3,"e":"In Tetralogy of Fallot, the degree of pulmonary infundibular (right ventricular outflow) stenosis determines right-to-left shunting; severity dictates cyanosis and prognosis.<br><br>Take Notes: VSD → present but not severity marker | Overriding aorta → fixed | TOF prognosis = pulmonary infundibular stenosis severity."},
/*35*/ {"q":"Which health model focuses on the patient's perception of their disease and the influence of the doctor's persuasion?","o":["Health Belief Model","Biopsychosocial Model","Social Cognitive Theory","Transtheoretical Model","Stages of Change Model"],"a":0,"e":"The Health Belief Model centres on patient's perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, and barriers; the doctor's persuasion influences these perceptions and adherence to treatment.<br><br>Take Notes: Biopsychosocial → biological + psychological + social | Transtheoretical → stages of change | Health Belief Model = patient perception + doctor persuasion."},
/*36*/ {"q":"Stress-related glycogenolysis in the liver is primarily stimulated by:","o":["Glucagon","Cortisol","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Insulin"],"a":2,"e":"In acute stress, epinephrine (adrenaline) from adrenal medulla acutely stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis via β2 receptors and cAMP, providing rapid glucose mobilisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon → mainly fasting glycogenolysis | Cortisol → gluconeogenesis | Stress glycogenolysis = epinephrine (fastest response)."},
/*37*/ {"q":"Severity of pancreatitis is best assessed by:","o":["Amylase 650, WBC 18,000, PaO2 65 mmHg","Amylase, LDH, and Albumin levels","Amylase 800, WBC elevated, and LDH 6.5","Serum lipase alone","Serum calcium alone"],"a":1,"e":"Ranson criteria and modified Glasgow scoring use multiple parameters (LDH, WBC, glucose, age, albumin); severity is assessed by composite scores, not single enzyme levels.<br><br>Take Notes: Amylase alone → diagnostic not prognostic | Lipase → diagnostic | Severity assessment = multiparametric (LDH + albumin + WBC)."},
/*38*/ {"q":"Aortic stenosis leads to which of the following?","o":["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Atrial fibrillation","Pulmonary hypertension","Right atrial dilation"],"a":0,"e":"Aortic stenosis creates pressure overload on the left ventricle, requiring greater systolic pressure to eject blood; this causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy as a compensatory adaptation.<br><br>Take Notes: Right ventricular hypertrophy → pulmonary stenosis | Atrial fib → mitral disease | Aortic stenosis = concentric LV hypertrophy."},
/*39*/ {"q":"A patient with Sheehan syndrome presents with dizziness, fatigue, and tiredness. Which hormone deficiency is primarily responsible for these symptoms?","o":["TSH","GnRH","Cortisol","ACTH","Growth hormone"],"a":3,"e":"Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis) causes anterior hypopituitarism; ACTH deficiency leads to secondary adrenal insufficiency, producing fatigue, hypotension, and dizziness as the most life-threatening deficit.<br><br>Take Notes: TSH loss → slow myxoedema | GH loss → subtle | Sheehan dizziness/fatigue = ACTH deficiency (cortisol)."},
/*40*/ {"q":"A patient presents with symptoms of hyperthyroidism along with a gritty sensation in the eyes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Graves' disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","Plummer's disease"],"a":0,"e":"Graves disease combines hyperthyroidism with infiltrative ophthalmopathy (proptosis, gritty eye sensation, lid lag) from autoantibody-mediated orbital inflammation; only Graves causes ocular involvement.<br><br>Take Notes: Toxic multinodular → no eye signs | Hashimoto → hypothyroid | Hyperthyroid + gritty eyes = Graves disease."},
/*41*/ {"q":"A patient was injected with FDG-18 (Fluorodeoxyglucose-18). Which radionuclear test is most likely being performed?","o":["Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scan","Thyroid scintigraphy","Bone scan (Tc-99m)","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan","SPECT scan"],"a":0,"e":"FDG-18 (fluorine-18 labelled glucose analog) is the standard radiotracer for PET imaging; metabolically active tissues (tumours, brain) take up FDG, emitting positrons for detection.<br><br>Take Notes: Tc-99m → bone scans | Iodine-123/131 → thyroid | FDG-18 = glucose analog = PET scan."},
/*42*/ {"q":"A 2-year-old child presents with diarrhoea and flattening of the brush border. Deficiency of which enzyme is most likely responsible?","o":["Lipase","Amylase","Enterokinase","Peptidase","Sucrase"],"a":2,"e":"Brush border flattening in young children causes congenital enterokinase deficiency or coeliac-like enteropathy; enterokinase activates trypsinogen, so its loss leads to malabsorption and diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipase/amylase → pancreatic | Peptidase → various | Brush border deficiency = enterokinase = trypsin activation failure."},
/*43*/ {"q":"Biosynthesis of which molecule is affected?","o":["Elastin","Collagen","Myosin","Fibrillin","Keratin"],"a":2,"e":"Among connective tissue proteins, myosin synthesis defects affect contractile function; in this context (likely muscular dystrophy reference), myosin biosynthesis impairment causes muscle weakness.<br><br>Take Notes: Elastin → arterial walls | Collagen → scurvy/OI | Fibrillin → Marfan | Muscle = myosin biosynthesis."},
/*44*/ {"q":"Glucocorticoids given in shock. Their beneficial role:","o":["Respiratory ways distension","Decreased heart rate","Increased vascular responsiveness to catecholamines","Reduction in inflammatory cytokines","Increased aldosterone secretion"],"a":2,"e":"In septic/anaphylactic shock, glucocorticoids upregulate adrenergic receptors and enhance vascular responsiveness to catecholamines, restoring vasomotor tone and blood pressure.<br><br>Take Notes: Anti-inflammatory effect → secondary in acute shock | Direct vasopressor → no | Shock benefit = vascular sensitisation to catecholamines."},
/*45*/ {"q":"Germ tube test positive by which fungal species?","o":["Candida albicans","Candida auris","Candida glabrata","Candida tropicalis","Candida krusei"],"a":0,"e":"The germ tube test identifies Candida albicans: it produces germ tubes (true hyphal projections without constriction at the base) within 2-3 hours in serum at 37°C.<br><br>Take Notes: C. auris/glabrata/tropicalis → germ tube negative | Cornmeal agar → chlamydospores | Germ tube positive = C. albicans."},
/*46*/ {"q":"After women underwent laparotomy, how much time will the scar gain near normal strength?","o":["1 year","6 months","3 months","Never","1 month"],"a":2,"e":"Wound healing remodelling phase: 80% tensile strength regained by 3 months. Full strength is never reached (max 80% of original). 3 months represents near-normal functional strength.<br><br>Take Notes: 1 week → 10% strength | 6 weeks → 60% | 3 months = ~80% (near normal)."},
/*47*/ {"q":"Specific immunity of the body is mediated by:","o":["Skin and mucous membranes","Lymphocytes","PMNs (polymorphonuclear cells)","Macrophages","Complement"],"a":1,"e":"Specific (adaptive) immunity is antigen-specific and memory-bearing, mediated by lymphocytes (B and T cells). Skin, PMNs, and macrophages are innate (non-specific) immunity components.<br><br>Take Notes: Skin/mucous membranes → innate barrier | PMNs/macrophages → innate phagocytic | Specific immunity = lymphocytes (B + T cells)."},
/*48*/ {"q":"CT of a fetus shows one large ventricle with fused thalami. The defect most likely lies in which embryological structure?","o":["Prosencephalon","Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Diancephalon","Metencephalon"],"a":0,"e":"Holoprosencephaly (single ventricle with fused thalami) results from failed cleavage of the prosencephalon (forebrain) at 5-6 weeks; severity ranges from alobar to lobar forms.<br><br>Take Notes: Mesencephalon → midbrain | Diencephalon → thalami (involved but downstream) | Holoprosencephaly = prosencephalon cleavage failure."},
/*49*/ {"q":"Instruments and packaging materials which cannot be steam sterilized are disinfected using which agent?","o":["Chlorine","Phenol","Cetylperidinium Chloride","Ethylene gas","Glutaraldehyde"],"a":3,"e":"Ethylene oxide gas is used for heat- and moisture-sensitive instruments and packaging materials (plastic, electronic devices) that cannot withstand steam sterilization. It is effective at low temperatures.<br><br>Take Notes: Chlorine/phenol → surface disinfection | Glutaraldehyde → liquid for endoscopes | Heat-sensitive items = ethylene oxide gas."},
/*50*/ {"q":"Which of the following causes an increase in extracellular potassium?","o":["Beta-2 agonist","Severe exercise","Excess aldosterone","Insulin deficiency","Alkalosis"],"a":3,"e":"Insulin promotes cellular K+ uptake via Na/K-ATPase; insulin deficiency (or DKA) shifts K+ extracellularly, raising serum potassium. Exercise causes transient release but is short-lived.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta-2 agonist/insulin → drive K+ into cells | Aldosterone → renal K+ excretion | Insulin deficiency = hyperkalemia."},
/*51*/ {"q":"A baby was spun around by the father and now presents with the hand in a semi-prone position while sitting. Injury to which joint is most likely?","o":["Proximal radioulnar joint","Distal radioulnar joint","Humeroulnar joint","Scaphoid joint","Elbow joint"],"a":0,"e":"\"Nursemaid's elbow\" (radial head subluxation) occurs when a child is jerked by the arm; the annular ligament slips over the radial head at the proximal radioulnar joint, causing semi-prone arm.<br><br>Take Notes: Distal radioulnar → wrist movement | Humeroulnar → flexion/extension | Pulled elbow = proximal radioulnar subluxation."},
/*52*/ {"q":"If the radius of a blood vessel is reduced to half of its original size due to a pre-occlusive valve, which parameter will increase 16 times?","o":["Resistance","Blood flow","Viscosity","Capillary pressure","Pressure gradient"],"a":0,"e":"Poiseuille's law: resistance is inversely proportional to radius^4. Halving radius (1/2)^4 = 1/16, so resistance increases 16 times. Flow decreases 16 times correspondingly.<br><br>Take Notes: Flow → decreases 16 times | Viscosity → independent of radius | Halved radius = 16x resistance increase (r^4 law)."},
/*53*/ {"q":"In anterior mandibular nerve damage, which muscle would escape paralysis?","o":["Digastric","Mylohyoid","Omohyoid","Medial Pterygoid","Tensor tympani"],"a":2,"e":"The anterior division of mandibular nerve (V3) supplies muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini. Omohyoid is supplied by ansa cervicalis (C1-C3), so it escapes.<br><br>Take Notes: Digastric (anterior)/mylohyoid → mandibular nerve | Medial pterygoid → mandibular | Omohyoid = ansa cervicalis = spared."},
/*54*/ {"q":"A patient underwent oophorectomy and was found to have salpingitis on examination. What is the epithelium of the affected organ?","o":["Simple columnar ciliated","Simple columnar non-ciliated","Simple cuboidal","Stratified epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar"],"a":0,"e":"The fallopian tube (affected in salpingitis) is lined by simple columnar ciliated epithelium with peg cells. Cilia transport ovum toward uterus; loss causes infertility and ectopic risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Uterus → simple columnar (non-ciliated mostly) | Vagina → stratified squamous | Fallopian tube = simple columnar ciliated."},
/*55*/ {"q":"A patient presents with decreased appetite and weight gain, and you prescribe an SSRI. Which of the following is the most bothersome side effect for the patient?","o":["Sexual dysfunction","Weight gain","Insomnia","Tachycardia","Headache"],"a":0,"e":"Sexual dysfunction (decreased libido, anorgasmia, delayed ejaculation) is the most common bothersome long-term SSRI side effect, affecting 30-70% of patients and reducing compliance.<br><br>Take Notes: Insomnia → early, transient | Weight gain → variable | SSRIs = sexual dysfunction most bothersome long-term."},
/*56*/ {"q":"How can malignant hyperthermia be differentiated from thyroid storm?","o":["Presence of tachycardia","Sudden rise in temperature","Malignant hyperthermia occurs only after anesthesia exposure","Elevated creatine kinase (CK) levels in malignant hyperthermia","Elevated TSH"],"a":2,"e":"Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by inhalational anesthetics or succinylcholine in genetically susceptible patients (RYR1 mutation); it occurs only with anaesthesia, unlike thyroid storm which has multiple triggers.<br><br>Take Notes: Both cause tachycardia/fever | CK elevated in MH (also rhabdomyolysis) | MH = anaesthesia trigger only."},
/*57*/ {"q":"A patient develops jaundice after taking herbal medication prescribed by hakeem. Liver biopsy reveals parenchymal necrosis. Which serum enzyme is most likely to be significantly elevated?","o":["Alanine aminotransferase (ALT)","Aspartate aminotransferase (AST)","Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)","Gamma glutamyl transferase (GGT)","Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)"],"a":0,"e":"ALT is highly specific for hepatocellular injury (parenchymal necrosis); in drug-induced liver injury (DILI) and hepatitis, ALT rises higher than AST due to its concentration in hepatocytes.<br><br>Take Notes: ALP/GGT → cholestasis | AST → less liver-specific | Hepatocellular necrosis = ALT (most specific)."},
/*58*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old patient is brought to the emergency department after a road traffic accident (RTA) leading to blunt trauma. He complains of difficulty breathing and decreased chest movements on the affected side. Chest X-ray reveals a radiolucent (black) shadow on one side. Rib fractures are also noted. What is the most likely cause of decreased chest movement?","o":["Pneumothorax","Fluid in the pleural cavity","Haemothorax","Lung abscess","Pulmonary contusion"],"a":0,"e":"Trauma with rib fractures and radiolucent (black) shadow on chest X-ray indicates pneumothorax (air in pleural space). Decreased breath sounds and chest expansion are characteristic.<br><br>Take Notes: Haemothorax → opaque (white) shadow | Fluid → also opaque | Radiolucent black = air = pneumothorax."},
/*59*/ {"q":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome is caused by compression of which of the following nerves?","o":["Lateral plantar nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Posterior tibial nerve","Anterior tibial nerve","Sural nerve"],"a":2,"e":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome: compression of the posterior tibial nerve (and its branches) beneath the flexor retinaculum behind the medial malleolus, causing burning pain and paraesthesia in the sole.<br><br>Take Notes: Anterior tibial → foot dorsum | Sural → lateral foot | Tarsal tunnel = posterior tibial nerve compression."},
/*60*/ {"q":"Which of the following base pair mutation occurs in sickle cell disease?","o":["Adenine to thymine","Thymine to adenine","Cytosine to guanine","Guanine to cytosine","Adenine to cytosine"],"a":0,"e":"Sickle cell disease results from a single point mutation in the β-globin gene: GAG (glutamate) → GTG (valine) at codon 6, representing an adenine to thymine DNA substitution.<br><br>Take Notes: Missense mutation | Glu→Val at position 6 | Sickle cell = adenine → thymine in β-globin codon 6."},
/*61*/ {"q":"Normal subocciput-bregmatic diameter is:","o":["9.5 cm","11 cm","13 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm"],"a":0,"e":"The suboccipitobregmatic diameter (smallest fetal head diameter, ~9.5 cm) is the presenting AP diameter in well-flexed vertex delivery; it represents the most favourable engagement diameter.<br><br>Take Notes: Occipitofrontal → 11.5 cm | Mentovertical → 14 cm (largest) | Suboccipitobregmatic = 9.5 cm (smallest, optimal)."},
/*62*/ {"q":"Methotrexate mechanism of action:","o":["Inhibit dihydrofolate reductase","Act as antimetabolite for para-aminobenzoic acid to decrease folic acid synthesis","Activate DNA","Inhibit proteins","Inhibit purine synthesis"],"a":0,"e":"Methotrexate is a folate analog that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate; this blocks thymidylate, purine, and amino acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Sulfonamides → PABA antagonism (bacterial) | TMP → bacterial DHFR | MTX = human DHFR inhibitor."},
/*63*/ {"q":"The sympathetic innervation to the lower respiratory tract arises from which spinal cord levels?","o":["T1 to T4","T5 to T7","T4, T5, T6","T8 to T9","T10 to T12"],"a":0,"e":"Sympathetic innervation of lower respiratory tract (bronchi, bronchioles) originates from upper thoracic spinal levels T1-T4 via sympathetic chain ganglia, mediating bronchodilation.<br><br>Take Notes: Parasympathetic (vagus) → bronchoconstriction | T5-T9 → upper GIT | Lower respiratory tract sympathetics = T1-T4."},
/*64*/ {"q":"What is site of tracheostomy?","o":["C2 ring","C3 ring","C4 ring","C5 ring","C6 ring"],"a":0,"e":"Tracheostomy in adults is performed at the 2nd-3rd tracheal ring (corresponding C2-C3 ring of trachea, NOT cervical vertebra). In children: at 3rd-4th tracheal ring to avoid cricoid injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Avoid 1st tracheal ring → cricoid damage | Adults C2-C3 | Children → lower (C3-C4) tracheal ring."},
/*65*/ {"q":"MI is related to which type of necrosis?","o":["Caseous necrosis","Liquefactive necrosis","Coagulative necrosis","Fibrinoid necrosis","Fat necrosis"],"a":2,"e":"Myocardial infarction shows coagulative necrosis: tissue architecture preserved with loss of cellular detail (anucleate cells, eosinophilic cytoplasm). Heart, kidney, spleen show this pattern.<br><br>Take Notes: Liquefactive → brain infarct/abscess | Caseous → TB | MI = coagulative necrosis (preserved architecture)."},
/*66*/ {"q":"A patient presented with cardiac, facial, and corneal defects along with hypotonia and failure to thrive, diagnosed as Zellweger syndrome in which there is accumulation of long-chain fatty acids. Caused by?","o":["Peroxisomes","Mitochondria","Lysosomes","Golgi body","Smooth ER"],"a":0,"e":"Zellweger syndrome is autosomal recessive peroxisome biogenesis disorder (PEX gene mutations) causing absent peroxisomes; very-long-chain fatty acids accumulate, with cardiac, facial, and neurological features.<br><br>Take Notes: Mitochondria → energy/oxidation | Lysosomes → degradation | Zellweger = peroxisome biogenesis defect."},
/*67*/ {"q":"A 16-year-old boy presented with self-mutilation, involuntary movements, speech problems, painful joints and urine shows excess uric acid. What is the deficient enzyme?","o":["Xanthine oxidase","HGPRT","Urate oxidase","Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase","ADA"],"a":1,"e":"Lesch-Nyhan syndrome: X-linked HGPRT deficiency causes hyperuricemia, gout, kidney stones, intellectual disability, choreoathetosis, and characteristic self-mutilation (lip biting, finger biting).<br><br>Take Notes: Xanthine oxidase → xanthinuria | ADA → SCID | Self-mutilation + hyperuricemia = HGPRT deficiency."},
/*68*/ {"q":"Hyaline cartilage under microscope appears transparent because of:","o":["Collagen fibers are visible under a light microscope","Elastic fibers are visible under a light microscope","Neither collagen nor elastic fibers are visible under a light microscope","Both collagen and elastic fibers are visible under a light microscope","Collagen fibers are not visible under a light microscope"],"a":2,"e":"Hyaline cartilage has thin, regularly arranged type II collagen fibrils whose refractive index matches the surrounding amorphous matrix, making both collagen and elastic fibers invisible under light microscopy.<br><br>Take Notes: Elastic cartilage → elastic fibers visible | Fibrocartilage → thick collagen visible | Hyaline = neither visible (glass-like)."},
/*69*/ {"q":"Demarcation of upper GIT from:","o":["Lower GIT is shown by ligament of Treitz","Lower GIT is shown by superior mesenteric artery","Lower GIT is shown by falciform ligament","Lower GIT is shown by tail of pancreas","Lower GIT is shown by hepatic flexure"],"a":0,"e":"The ligament of Treitz (suspensory ligament of duodenum) marks the duodenojejunal junction and divides upper GIT (proximal to it, foregut) from lower GIT (distal, midgut).<br><br>Take Notes: SMA → arterial supply | Falciform → liver | Upper/lower GIT = ligament of Treitz at DJ flexure."},
/*70*/ {"q":"Biceps femoris nerve supply is by:","o":["Tibial and Common Peroneal (CPN)","Tibial only","Common peroneal only","Sciatic only","Femoral nerve"],"a":0,"e":"Biceps femoris has dual innervation: long head by tibial division of sciatic nerve; short head by common peroneal division. Distinct nerve supply to each head is unique to biceps femoris.<br><br>Take Notes: Other hamstrings → tibial division only | Quadriceps → femoral | Biceps femoris = tibial + common peroneal."},
/*71*/ {"q":"A patient presents with a swelling on the posterior aspect of the hand that becomes prominent on wrist flexion. There are no signs of inflammation, and the transillumination test is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Lipoma","Ganglion cyst","Abscess","Cellulitis","Sebaceous cyst"],"a":1,"e":"A ganglion cyst is a fluid-filled (mucinous) cystic swelling arising from tendon sheath or joint capsule, typically on the dorsal wrist; it transilluminates and becomes prominent on flexion.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipoma → soft, doesn't transilluminate | Abscess → tender, inflamed | Dorsal wrist + transillumination = ganglion cyst."},
/*72*/ {"q":"Which of the following muscles attached on lesser trochanter of femur?","o":["Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimus","Gluteus medius","Iliopsoas muscle","Tensor fasciae latae"],"a":3,"e":"The iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus) inserts on the lesser trochanter of femur; it is the principal hip flexor. Gluteal muscles insert on greater trochanter or iliotibial tract.<br><br>Take Notes: Gluteus maximus → gluteal tuberosity/IT band | Gluteus medius/minimus → greater trochanter | Lesser trochanter = iliopsoas."},
/*73*/ {"q":"What is the name of the nucleus responsible for detecting phone vibrations in the pocket?","o":["Nucleus Proprius","Nucleus Dorsalis","Vestibular Nucleus","Mesencephalic Nucleus of the Trigeminal Nerve","Nucleus Cuneatus"],"a":1,"e":"Nucleus dorsalis (Clarke's column) at T1-L2 receives proprioceptive and vibration input from lower body; it relays to cerebellum via dorsal spinocerebellar tract for vibration sensation.<br><br>Take Notes: Vestibular nucleus → balance | Mesencephalic V → jaw proprioception | Body vibration sense = nucleus dorsalis."},
/*74*/ {"q":"A patient is having rhinosinusitis and nasal polyp with the fungus invading lamina papyracea, excessive endothelial damage and having non-septate hyphae branching at right angle. Organism involved is:","o":["Candida","Histoplasmosis","Aspergillus","Mucor","Cryptococcus"],"a":3,"e":"Non-septate (aseptate) broad hyphae branching at right (90°) angles invading sinuses with vascular damage indicates Mucor (mucormycosis). Common in diabetic ketoacidosis and immunocompromised patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Aspergillus → septate, acute (45°) angle branching | Candida → yeast/pseudohyphae | Mucor = non-septate + 90° branching."},
/*75*/ {"q":"A 25-year-old male with swelling that is lateral and inferior to pubic tubercle with positive cough impulse has:","o":["Inguinal hernia","Femoral hernia","Umbilical hernia","Incisional hernia","Spigelian hernia"],"a":1,"e":"A femoral hernia presents below and lateral to the pubic tubercle (vs. inguinal which is above and medial); it emerges through the femoral canal, more common in females.<br><br>Take Notes: Inguinal → above + medial to pubic tubercle | Umbilical → midline | Below + lateral pubic tubercle = femoral hernia."},
/*76*/ {"q":"A patient presented with dry skin. Investigations reveal anti-thyroid peroxidase (TPO) antibodies. Antibodies are commonly found in which of the following conditions?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","Subacute thyroiditis","Graves' disease"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-TPO antibodies are the hallmark serological marker for Hashimoto thyroiditis (chronic autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis), present in >90% of cases; they target thyroid peroxidase enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: RA → RF, anti-CCP | SLE → ANA, anti-dsDNA | Anti-TPO = Hashimoto thyroiditis."},
/*77*/ {"q":"ECF osmolarity is regulated by:","o":["Cortisol","GH","ADH","TSH","Aldosterone"],"a":2,"e":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH/vasopressin) from posterior pituitary regulates ECF osmolarity by controlling renal water reabsorption via V2 receptors and aquaporin-2 channels in collecting ducts.<br><br>Take Notes: Aldosterone → ECF volume/sodium | Cortisol → stress response | ECF osmolarity = ADH (vasopressin)."},
/*78*/ {"q":"A young male presents with sudden onset of memory loss. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus","Alzheimer's Disease","Temporal lobe epilepsy","Wernicke's encephalopathy","Transient global amnesia"],"a":4,"e":"Transient global amnesia: sudden anterograde amnesia in middle-aged adults lasting <24 hours, often triggered by stress, exertion, or temperature change. Spontaneous recovery is typical.<br><br>Take Notes: Alzheimer = insidious, chronic | NPH = elderly + gait + incontinence | Sudden young memory loss = transient global amnesia."},
/*79*/ {"q":"A 27-year-old woman requests a mammogram because both her mother and sister died of metastatic breast cancer before 40 years of age. Which of the following would add to this patient's risk for breast cancer?","o":["Multiparity","High-fiber diet","OCPs","BRCA1 mutation","Bilateral fibroadenomas"],"a":3,"e":"BRCA1 mutation confers 55-65% lifetime breast cancer risk and tends to present early (<40 years); strong family history of premenopausal breast cancer in first-degree relatives suggests hereditary BRCA1.<br><br>Take Notes: Multiparity/high-fiber → protective | OCPs → minor risk | Strong family history + early onset = BRCA1 mutation."},
/*80*/ {"q":"A 40-year-old female with no respiratory or cardiac problems has swelling in front of the neck and feels difficulty breathing while in supine position. This is due to:","o":["Asthma","Colloid goiter","Follicular carcinoma of goiter","Retrosternal goiter","Multinodular goiter"],"a":3,"e":"Retrosternal goiter extends below thoracic inlet, compressing the trachea more in supine position (gravity reduces); causes orthopnea-like dyspnea in supine, relieved by sitting up.<br><br>Take Notes: Asthma → bronchospasm, wheezing | Simple goiter → no positional dyspnoea | Supine dyspnoea + neck swelling = retrosternal goiter."},
/*81*/ {"q":"A young female having bilateral ovarian mass on histopath mass consist of skin, appendages, epithelial cells and mucous glands. Diagnosis?","o":["Sex cord tumor","Endodermal sinus tumor","Teratoma","Dysgerminoma","Choriocarcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) contains tissues from all three germ layers: skin, appendages (sebaceous glands, hair), epithelial cells, and mucous glands. Most common ovarian germ cell tumor.<br><br>Take Notes: Dysgerminoma → seminoma-like, single cell type | Endodermal sinus → Schiller-Duvall | Multi-germ-layer ovarian = teratoma."},
/*82*/ {"q":"A 2-year-old child with painless swelling in testicular region underwent orchiectomy. Biopsy showed cuboidal cells eosinophilic in nature, non-specific immature tubuloid glands with thin sheaths and glomeruloid structure. Diagnosis?","o":["Yolk sac tumor","Teratoma","Seminoma","Dysgerminoma","Leydig cell tumor"],"a":0,"e":"Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor is the most common testicular tumor in children <3 years; histology shows Schiller-Duval bodies (glomeruloid structures), reticular pattern, and cuboidal eosinophilic cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Seminoma → adults | Teratoma → multi-germ-layer | Child <3y + Schiller-Duvall = yolk sac tumor (Robbins)."},
/*83*/ {"q":"Medullary CA tumor marker is:","o":["CA 125","CA 19-9","Calcitonin","CA 15-5","ALP"],"a":2,"e":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells that secrete calcitonin; serum calcitonin is the specific tumor marker, used for diagnosis and post-operative monitoring.<br><br>Take Notes: CA 125 → ovarian | CA 19-9 → pancreatic | CA 15-3 → breast | Medullary thyroid CA = calcitonin."},
/*84*/ {"q":"VMA in urine seen in:","o":["Pheochromocytoma","Alkaptonuria","Glycogen storage disease","Lysosomal disorder","Carcinoid syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) is the final metabolite of catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine); elevated urinary VMA is diagnostic for pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.<br><br>Take Notes: Alkaptonuria → homogentisic acid | Carcinoid → 5-HIAA | Pheochromocytoma = elevated urinary VMA + metanephrines."},
/*85*/ {"q":"27-year-old woman presents with weight gain, menorrhoea, and fatigue. Last year, she experienced a complicated delivery requiring 2 units of blood transfusion. She was unable to breastfeed. On examination she has dry skin and hypotension. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Conn disease","Sheehan syndrome","Addison syndrome","Nelson syndrome","Hashimoto's thyroiditis"],"a":1,"e":"Sheehan syndrome: postpartum pituitary necrosis from severe haemorrhage causing anterior hypopituitarism. Failure to lactate (prolactin loss) is early sign; weight gain, hypotension, dry skin from TSH/ACTH deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Conn → hyperaldosteronism | Addison → primary adrenal | Postpartum + no lactation = Sheehan syndrome."},
/*86*/ {"q":"A woman with history of severe postpartum hemorrhage presents with weakness, dizziness, and inability to lactate. What hormone deficiencies are expected?","o":["ACTH","TSH","GH","Prolactin","All anterior pituitary hormones"],"a":0,"e":"Sheehan syndrome causes loss of all anterior pituitary hormones. ACTH deficiency causing secondary adrenal insufficiency (cortisol deficiency) produces weakness, dizziness, hypotension - the most immediately life-threatening.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolactin → failed lactation (early sign) | TSH → myxoedema (late) | Acute symptoms = ACTH/cortisol deficiency."},
/*87*/ {"q":"Injury to medial epicondyle leading to wasting of small muscles in hand along with lateral sparing indicates injury to which nerve?","o":["Radial Nerve","Median Nerve","Ulnar Nerve","Musculocutaneous Nerve","Axillary Nerve"],"a":2,"e":"Ulnar nerve passes posterior to medial epicondyle (vulnerable to fracture); supplies most intrinsic hand muscles. Lateral sparing (thenar muscles spared) is due to preserved median nerve innervation.<br><br>Take Notes: Median nerve → thenar (lateral) muscles | Radial → wrist extension | Medial epicondyle injury = ulnar nerve."},
/*88*/ {"q":"In the Rh blood group system, Rh agglutinins:","o":["Are predominantly IgM","Are present on the erythrocyte cell membrane","Are always present in Rh-negative individuals","Secreted in saliva","Are predominantly IgG"],"a":4,"e":"Rh agglutinins (antibodies) are predominantly IgG, developing after exposure (transfusion, pregnancy); they cross the placenta and cause hemolytic disease of the newborn in subsequent Rh-incompatible pregnancies.<br><br>Take Notes: ABO antibodies = IgM, natural | Rh antigen = on RBC membrane | Rh antibodies = IgG, immune-mediated."},
/*89*/ {"q":"A female baby with enlarged clitoris and absent uterus on USG. This feature is related to:","o":["Down Syndrome","Complete mole","Adrenogenital syndrome","Partial mole","Turner syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Adrenogenital syndrome (congenital adrenal hyperplasia, 21-hydroxylase deficiency) in 46,XX females causes virilisation with clitoromegaly. Absent uterus suggests 46,XY DSD variant or severe ambiguity.<br><br>Take Notes: Down → trisomy 21, no virilisation | Turner → 45,X, female phenotype | Clitoromegaly = adrenogenital syndrome (CAH)."},
/*90*/ {"q":"Truncal vagotomy causes:","o":["Decrease gastric motility","Decrease gastric acid emptying","Decrease pepsin secretion","None of above","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Truncal vagotomy denervates all parasympathetic supply to abdominal viscera; reduces gastric acid secretion, pepsin secretion, gastric motility, and gastric emptying (delays emptying causing dumping syndrome).<br><br>Take Notes: Selective vagotomy → spares gastric motility | Truncal = all parasympathetic loss | Truncal vagotomy = decreased motility + acid + pepsin."},
/*91*/ {"q":"Left circumflex artery blocked. What will be affected?","o":["Left atrium and left ventricle","Right atrium","Right ventricle","Right atrium and right ventricle","Left atrium only"],"a":0,"e":"Left circumflex artery (branch of left coronary) supplies left atrium and posterolateral wall of left ventricle, including SA node in 40% of patients. Occlusion causes left-sided ischemia.<br><br>Take Notes: RCA → right atrium, right ventricle, inferior LV | LAD → anterior LV/septum | LCX = left atrium + lateral LV wall."},
/*92*/ {"q":"Type of fluid due to appendicitis is:","o":["Exudative","Transudative","Serous","Fibrinous","Purulent"],"a":0,"e":"Inflammation in appendicitis produces exudative fluid: high protein content (>2.5 g/dL), high specific gravity (>1.020), and inflammatory cells from increased vascular permeability.<br><br>Take Notes: Transudate → CHF, low protein | Serous → mild inflammation | Appendicitis = exudative fluid (inflammatory)."},
/*93*/ {"q":"In a cardiac patient which of the following is the cause of edema?","o":["Increase Plasma Oncotic pressure","Decrease Hydrostatic pressure","Increase Hydrostatic pressure","Decrease vascular permeability","Decrease Plasma Oncotic pressure"],"a":2,"e":"In cardiac failure (CHF), reduced cardiac output leads to fluid backup; increased venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure drives fluid into interstitium, causing peripheral edema.<br><br>Take Notes: Decreased oncotic pressure → nephrotic/liver edema | Vascular permeability → inflammatory edema | Cardiac edema = increased hydrostatic pressure."},
/*94*/ {"q":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy presents with hematuria. Which of the following drugs is most likely responsible for this presentation?","o":["Cyclosporine","Cyclophosphamide","Doxorubicin","Azithromycin","Bleomycin"],"a":1,"e":"Cyclophosphamide metabolite acrolein damages bladder urothelium, causing hemorrhagic cystitis with painful hematuria. Prevented and treated with mesna (sulfhydryl compound that binds acrolein) and adequate hydration.<br><br>Take Notes: Doxorubicin → cardiotoxicity (DCM) | Bleomycin → pulmonary fibrosis | Cyclophosphamide = hemorrhagic cystitis from acrolein."},
/*95*/ {"q":"CCl4 causes damage to cells by which of the following mechanism?","o":["Oxygen","Free radicals production","Apoptosis","Fatty change","Direct toxicity"],"a":1,"e":"Carbon tetrachloride (CCl4) metabolised by P450 in hepatocytes to CCl3 free radical; this triggers lipid peroxidation of cell membranes, ER damage, and fatty change in liver.<br><br>Take Notes: Apoptosis → programmed cell death (no inflammation) | Fatty change → consequence not mechanism | CCl4 = free radical-induced damage."},
/*96*/ {"q":"A patient develops muscle pain after starting an antihyperlipidemic drug. Which of the following is most likely responsible?","o":["Statins","Fibrates","Ezetimibe","Bile acid sequestrants","Niacin"],"a":0,"e":"Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) cause myopathy/myalgia in 5-10% of patients via mitochondrial dysfunction and reduced ubiquinone synthesis; severe form is rhabdomyolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Ezetimibe → GI side effects | Bile sequestrants → constipation | Antilipidemic + muscle pain = statins."},
/*97*/ {"q":"Thymoma is associated with myasthenia gravis. What other disease is associated with it?","o":["Pure Red Cell Aplasia","Aplastic Anemia","Eaton-Lambert Syndrome","Megaloblastic Anemia","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"a":0,"e":"Thymoma is associated with pure red cell aplasia (5-10% of thymomas), myasthenia gravis (30-50%), and hypogammaglobulinemia. Pure RBC aplasia is the classic non-MG paraneoplastic association.<br><br>Take Notes: Eaton-Lambert → small cell lung CA | Aplastic anemia → drugs/idiopathic | Thymoma = pure red cell aplasia + MG."},
/*98*/ {"q":"A patient presents with hypotension and hypoglycemia. Most probable cause?","o":["Autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex","Ectopic production of ACTH from a lung tumor","Excessive production of cortisol from a tumor in the adrenal cortex","Excessive production of aldosterone from a tumor in the adrenal cortex","Pituitary adenoma"],"a":0,"e":"Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) most commonly results from autoimmune destruction of adrenal cortex in developed countries; cortisol deficiency causes hypotension, hypoglycaemia, hyperpigmentation.<br><br>Take Notes: Ectopic ACTH → Cushing (high cortisol) | Aldosterone excess → hypertension | Addison = autoimmune adrenal destruction."},
/*99*/ {"q":"A patient underwent jejunal resection 4 months ago, they present with weakness, low hemoglobin, and an MCV >100 fL. Which of the following is the most likely cause of their anemia?","o":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency","Iron deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease"],"a":1,"e":"Folate is absorbed in proximal jejunum; jejunal resection rapidly depletes body stores (only 3-4 months reserve), causing megaloblastic macrocytic anaemia within months.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 = ileum, years of reserve | Iron = duodenum, microcytic | Jejunal resection + macrocytic 4 months = folate deficiency."},
/*100*/ {"q":"Atrial fibrillation Related ECG findings are which of following?","o":["Absent P-Waves","Irregular T-R Interval","Regular R-R Interval","Prominent QRS","Narrow P-R interval"],"a":0,"e":"Atrial fibrillation is characterised by absent P waves (replaced by fibrillatory waves), irregularly irregular R-R intervals, and normal QRS morphology unless conduction abnormalities exist.<br><br>Take Notes: Regular R-R → not AFib (AFib is irregular) | Prominent QRS → other conditions | AFib hallmark = absent P waves + irregularly irregular."},
/*101*/ {"q":"Lymphatic drainage of breast upper outer lateral quadrant is to:","o":["Posterior axillary","Central axillary","External axillary","Anterior axillary","Internal thoracic"],"a":3,"e":"The upper outer breast quadrant (most common cancer site) drains primarily to anterior (pectoral) axillary lymph nodes — the typical sentinel node group accessed in axillary clearance.<br><br>Take Notes: Internal mammary = medial breast | Posterior axillary = upper limb | Upper outer quadrant = anterior axillary."},
/*102*/ {"q":"Patent ductus arteriosus is associated with:","o":["Maternal age","Prematurity","Drugs","CO2","Down syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"PDA is strongly associated with prematurity due to immature pulmonary vasculature and prostaglandin sensitivity. Indomethacin closes PDA; PGE1 maintains patency in duct-dependent lesions.<br><br>Take Notes: Maternal rubella = congenital | Down syndrome = AVSD link | PDA = prematurity (most common association)."},
/*103*/ {"q":"Which one is autosomal dominant disorder?","o":["Sickle cell anemia","Thalassemia","Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)","Hemophilia","Cystic fibrosis"],"a":2,"e":"FAP is autosomal dominant; APC gene mutation causes >100 colon polyps in adolescence, with nearly 100% malignant transformation by age 40 if untreated by prophylactic colectomy.<br><br>Take Notes: Sickle/thalassemia = autosomal recessive | Hemophilia = X-linked | FAP = autosomal dominant (APC)."},
/*104*/ {"q":"Patient on ATT with big toe swelling most likely due to:","o":["Rifampicin","Pyrazinamide","INH","Ethambutol","Streptomycin"],"a":1,"e":"Pyrazinamide inhibits renal tubular uric acid secretion causing hyperuricemia, precipitating acute gout (classically big toe/first MTP — podagra) in susceptible individuals.<br><br>Take Notes: INH = peripheral neuropathy | Ethambutol = optic neuritis | Rifampicin = orange fluids | Big toe gout = pyrazinamide."},
/*105*/ {"q":"Side effects of antitubercular drugs:","o":["Isoniazid - Peripheral neuropathy","Rifampicin - Red/Orange Urine","Ethambutol - Optic Neuritis","Pyrazinamide - Hyperuricemia","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All listed associations are correct: INH causes peripheral neuropathy (B6 deficiency), rifampicin colors body fluids orange-red, ethambutol causes optic neuritis (red-green colour blindness), pyrazinamide causes hyperuricemia.<br><br>Take Notes: RIPE mnemonic = drug-specific toxicities | Each correct | All of the above = right answer."},
/*106*/ {"q":"A 10-week pregnant woman presents with persistent nausea, vomiting and noticeable weight loss. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Gout","Hyperemesis gravidarum","Gastroenteritis","Molar pregnancy","Urinary tract infection"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperemesis gravidarum: severe persistent vomiting in pregnancy (<16 weeks) causing >5% weight loss, dehydration, ketonuria, electrolyte disturbances. Linked to high hCG and first pregnancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Molar pregnancy = snowstorm USG | Gastroenteritis = diarrhoea | 10w pregnant + vomiting + weight loss = hyperemesis gravidarum."},
/*107*/ {"q":"A 48-year-old lady with late-stage CA fibroid asks you not to tell her family. Her son asks about her condition. What will you do?","o":["Tell something","Tell nothing","Inform the son","Inform the whole family","Refer to ethics committee"],"a":1,"e":"Patient confidentiality is paramount; without explicit patient consent, information cannot be disclosed to family. Respect autonomy and the patient's clearly expressed wishes.<br><br>Take Notes: Family-centred care needs patient consent | Confidentiality breach = exceptional | Patient refusal = tell nothing."},
/*108*/ {"q":"Female patient with webbed neck and small ears. Best way to diagnose?","o":["Bart body","Karyotyping","Ultrasound","Chronic villus sampling","Buccal smear"],"a":1,"e":"Webbed neck and low-set ears suggest Turner syndrome (45,X) or another chromosomal anomaly. Karyotyping is definitive, showing 45,X or mosaicism (45,X/46,XX).<br><br>Take Notes: Barr body = X-inactivation screening | USG = indirect | Buccal smear = limited | Chromosomal = karyotyping."},
/*109*/ {"q":"A 10-year-old boy presents after a bicycle accident and possible pelvic fracture. While awaiting CT scan, a physician proceeds with a focal neurologic examination. In testing the child's reflexes, which of the following nerves would carry afferent impulses of the cremasteric reflex?","o":["Subcostal Nerve","Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve","Genitofemoral Nerve","Iliohypogastric Nerve","Femoral Nerve"],"a":2,"e":"The cremasteric reflex (L1-L2): afferent limb via the ilioinguinal/genitofemoral nerve sensory branch; efferent via genital branch of genitofemoral to cremaster muscle. Stroking inner thigh elevates testicle.<br><br>Take Notes: Subcostal = T12 | Iliohypogastric = motor abdominal wall | Cremasteric afferent = genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)."},
/*110*/ {"q":"How to know if pelvis is female pelvis?","o":["Everted Iliac Ala","Heart Shaped Pelvis","Inverted Ischial tuberosities","Fused ischial spines at the Mid","Subpubic angle <90°"],"a":0,"e":"Female pelvis is gynaecoid: wider, shallower, with everted iliac alae, oval inlet, wider subpubic angle (>90°), shorter sacrum, and everted ischial tuberosities for childbirth.<br><br>Take Notes: Heart-shaped pelvis = android (male) | Subpubic angle <90° = male | Female = everted iliac ala."},
/*111*/ {"q":"An old man presents with tremors during fine work, gait abnormality, and past pointing on finger-nose test. Lesion is in:","o":["Midbrain","Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Pons","Substantia nigra"],"a":1,"e":"Intention tremor (worse with movement), gait ataxia, and past-pointing/dysmetria are cardinal cerebellar signs. Cerebellar lesions cause coordination disturbances, not the resting tremor of Parkinson's.<br><br>Take Notes: Substantia nigra = resting tremor (Parkinson) | Pons = pinpoint pupils | Intention tremor + ataxia = cerebellum."},
/*112*/ {"q":"A patient with severe headache, vomiting, neck stiffness, raised ICP. Lumbar puncture shows blood-stained CSF. Diagnosis:","o":["Subdural hemorrhage","Cerebral hemorrhage","Extradural hematoma","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Intraparenchymal hemorrhage"],"a":3,"e":"Subarachnoid haemorrhage presents with thunderclap headache, meningism, and uniformly blood-stained CSF on lumbar puncture. Often due to ruptured berry aneurysm at Circle of Willis.<br><br>Take Notes: Subdural/extradural = on CT, no SAH features | Cerebral = focal deficit | Blood in CSF + meningism = SAH."},
/*113*/ {"q":"Most specific marker in MI within 2 hours:","o":["CK-MB","Trop T","LDH","Trop I","Myoglobin"],"a":0,"e":"Within 2 hours of MI, CK-MB is the first cardiac-specific marker to rise (peaks at 24h). Myoglobin rises earlier but is non-specific; troponins rise after 3-6 hours.<br><br>Take Notes: Myoglobin = 1-2h, non-specific | Trop I = after 4-6h, most specific overall | At 2h = CK-MB."},
/*114*/ {"q":"Intrahepatic protein anabolism and extra hepatic catabolism:","o":["Glucocorticoid","Glucagon","Epinephrine","Growth hormone","Insulin"],"a":0,"e":"Glucocorticoids (cortisol) cause hepatic protein anabolism (increased gluconeogenic enzymes) while inducing extra-hepatic catabolism (muscle proteolysis releasing amino acids for gluconeogenesis).<br><br>Take Notes: GH = general anabolism | Insulin = anabolic | Glucagon = catabolic | Glucocorticoid = liver anabolic + peripheral catabolic."},
/*115*/ {"q":"A patient presents with arthritis, joint pain and morning stiffness along with kidney deposits, CCP raised and ANA negative. There is a precursor protein which stains red on Congo stain. What is the precursor protein?","o":["Serum amyloid precursor protein","Serum amyloid associated protein (SAA)","Lambda light chain","Transthyretin","Beta 2 microglobulin"],"a":1,"e":"In secondary (AA) amyloidosis, chronic inflammatory disease (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis with CCP+) elevates serum amyloid-associated (SAA) protein, which deposits as AA amyloid in kidneys.<br><br>Take Notes: AL amyloid = lambda light chain (multiple myeloma) | TTR = senile cardiac | RA + amyloid = SAA precursor."},
/*116*/ {"q":"ABGs of a patient reveal pH 7.4, pCO2 60 & HCO3 24. Acid-base abnormality?","o":["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Mixed alkalosis"],"a":0,"e":"PCO2 elevated (60, normal 40) with normal HCO3 indicates acute respiratory acidosis. Normal pH at 7.4 suggests compensation has begun, but raised PCO2 with normal bicarbonate = respiratory acidosis.<br><br>Take Notes: PCO2 high + HCO3 normal = respiratory acidosis | Metabolic = HCO3 change | pH 7.4 with raised PCO2 = compensated."},
/*117*/ {"q":"Congenital erythropoietic porphyria. What is finding in urine?","o":["Increase urine uroporphyrin 1","Stool porphyrin increase","Decrease urine porphyrin","Increase fecal + urinary + serum uroporphyrin 1","Normal porphyrins"],"a":3,"e":"Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (Günther's disease): defective uroporphyrinogen III synthase causes accumulation of uroporphyrin I in urine, faeces, and serum, with red urine and severe photosensitivity.<br><br>Take Notes: Acute intermittent porphyria = ALA + PBG | PCT = uroporphyrin urine | Congenital = uroporphyrin I in urine + stool + serum."},
/*118*/ {"q":"A patient presents with foot drop and inability to dorsiflex after a knee joint injury. Which nerve is damaged?","o":["Superficial peroneal Nerve","Deep Peroneal Nerve","Tibial Nerve","Sciatic Nerve","Sural Nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Deep peroneal nerve supplies tibialis anterior (dorsiflexor); injury near fibular neck causes foot drop. Compression at fibular neck after knee injury or prolonged crossing-legs.<br><br>Take Notes: Superficial peroneal = eversion + lateral sensation | Tibial = plantarflexion | Foot drop = deep peroneal nerve."},
/*119*/ {"q":"Demarcation of upper GIT from lower GIT is shown by:","o":["Lower GIT is shown by Lower GIT","Lower GIT is shown by Ligament of Treitz","Superior mesenteric artery","Ligament of Treitz (Falciform ligament)","Tail of pancreas"],"a":1,"e":"The ligament of Treitz (suspensory ligament of duodenum) marks the duodenojejunal flexure, dividing foregut/upper GIT (proximal) from midgut/lower GIT (distal). Useful in localising GI bleeds.<br><br>Take Notes: SMA = arterial supply, not landmark | Falciform = liver attachment | Upper/lower GIT = Ligament of Treitz."},
/*120*/ {"q":"Cell membrane soluble in:","o":["Soap water","Alcohol","Lipids","Proteins","Detergent only"],"a":2,"e":"Cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer; lipid-soluble (lipophilic) substances dissolve and cross it freely. Lipid solvents like ether, chloroform disrupt membranes by dissolving them.<br><br>Take Notes: Water-soluble molecules = need transporters/channels | Proteins = embedded in membrane | Cell membrane = soluble in lipids."},
/*121*/ {"q":"A patient has liver cirrhosis. Route of hepatic biopsy is:","o":["T10 midaxillary line","T11 mid clavicular line","Subcostal angle","Substernal angle","T9 anterior axillary"],"a":0,"e":"Percutaneous liver biopsy is performed in the right intercostal space at T10 mid-axillary line during expiration; this approach minimises risk to pleura, lung, and other organs.<br><br>Take Notes: Subcostal = risks bowel | T11 clavicular = pneumothorax risk | Liver biopsy = T10 midaxillary line."},
/*122*/ {"q":"Pregnant female with labour pain for 20 hours was admitted in emergency, being prepared for C-section, suddenly dies due to:","o":["Air embolism","Fat embolism","Amniotic fluid embolism","Pulmonary thromboembolism","Anaphylaxis"],"a":2,"e":"Amniotic fluid embolism: rare obstetric emergency where amniotic fluid enters maternal circulation during labour, causing acute cardiopulmonary collapse, DIC, and high mortality. Occurs during prolonged labour or C-section.<br><br>Take Notes: PE = post-op risk | Fat = long bone fracture | Sudden death in labour = amniotic fluid embolism."},
/*123*/ {"q":"Ansa cervicalis root value:","o":["Hypoglossal with C123","Hypoglossal with C34","Hypoglossal with C67","Hypoglossal","C1 alone"],"a":0,"e":"Ansa cervicalis is formed by fibers from C1, C2, C3 ventral rami; superior root descends with hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), then leaves it to join inferior root (C2-C3).<br><br>Take Notes: Phrenic nerve = C3,4,5 | Cervical plexus = C1-C4 | Ansa cervicalis = C1, C2, C3 (with hypoglossal)."},
/*124*/ {"q":"In the region of the submandibular gland, the lingual artery is lateral to which of the following structures?","o":["Hypoglossus muscle","Facial artery","Hyoglossus muscle","Genioglossus muscle","Styloglossus muscle"],"a":2,"e":"The lingual artery passes deep (medial) to hyoglossus muscle, while hypoglossal nerve crosses superficial (lateral) to hyoglossus. Lingual artery is thus lateral to hyoglossus in this region.<br><br>Take Notes: Hypoglossal nerve = superficial to hyoglossus | Lingual artery = deep | Lingual artery lateral to = hyoglossus muscle."},
/*125*/ {"q":"Turner syndrome:","o":["Complete Monosomy of X","Incomplete monosomy","Trinucleotide repeat","Complete Monosomy of Y","Trisomy X"],"a":0,"e":"Turner syndrome is 45,X (complete monosomy of X chromosome), characterised by short stature, webbed neck, primary amenorrhea, streak ovaries, cardiac defects (coarctation), and lymphoedema.<br><br>Take Notes: Trisomy X = 47,XXX (Triple X) | Trinucleotide = Fragile X/Huntington | Turner = complete X monosomy (45,X)."},
/*126*/ {"q":"Which hormone is inhibited by a factor released from the hypothalamus?","o":["Prolactin","Growth Hormone (GH)","Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH)","Oxytocin","TSH"],"a":0,"e":"Prolactin is uniquely under tonic inhibition by hypothalamic dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor); other anterior pituitary hormones are positively regulated by hypothalamic releasing hormones.<br><br>Take Notes: GH = released by GHRH | ADH/oxytocin = posterior pituitary | Prolactin = inhibited by dopamine."},
/*127*/ {"q":"If the superior nasal meatus is blocked by a polyp, which sinus drainage will be most affected?","o":["Posterior ethmoidal sinus","Maxillary sinus","Frontal sinus","Temporal sinus","Sphenoid sinus"],"a":0,"e":"The superior nasal meatus drains the posterior ethmoidal sinuses; blockage by polyp impedes drainage causing posterior ethmoiditis. Sphenoid drains into spheno-ethmoidal recess above superior meatus.<br><br>Take Notes: Maxillary/frontal/anterior ethmoid = middle meatus | Sphenoid = spheno-ethmoidal recess | Superior meatus = posterior ethmoidal sinus."},
/*128*/ {"q":"During suprapubic paracentesis for ascites with an empty bladder, the patient developed bleeding due to vascular injury. Which vessel is most likely to have been injured?","o":["Superior vesical artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Obturator artery","External pudendal artery","Femoral artery"],"a":1,"e":"Inferior epigastric artery runs deep to rectus abdominis lateral to midline; suprapubic procedures (paracentesis, port placement) can injure it. Empty bladder doesn't protect against this vessel.<br><br>Take Notes: Superior vesical = pelvic | Obturator = inferior pelvis | Suprapubic procedure = inferior epigastric injury."},
/*129*/ {"q":"A child presents with hepatosplenomegaly and pancytopenia. Histopathology reveals \"crumbled tissue paper\" appearance. It is due to accumulation of:","o":["Glucocerebroside","Sphingomyelinase","Ganglioside","Galactocerebroside","Alpha 1-4 amylase"],"a":0,"e":"Gaucher disease (most common lysosomal storage disease): glucocerebrosidase deficiency causes glucocerebroside accumulation in macrophages (Gaucher cells with \"crumpled tissue paper\" cytoplasm) - hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia.<br><br>Take Notes: Niemann-Pick = sphingomyelin | Tay-Sachs = GM2 ganglioside | Gaucher = glucocerebroside accumulation."},
/*130*/ {"q":"Sulphur granules containing:","o":["Staph aureus","Streptococcus","Actinomycetes","Listeria","Nocardia"],"a":2,"e":"Sulphur granules (yellow/orange firm granules) are characteristic of Actinomyces israelii infection; seen in actinomycosis with chronic suppurative draining sinus tracts in cervicofacial, thoracic, or abdominal regions.<br><br>Take Notes: Nocardia = acid-fast (weakly), aerobic | Actinomyces = anaerobic, sulphur granules | Sulphur granules = Actinomyces."},
/*131*/ {"q":"Baby born with cleft plate, ear and facial abnormalities. Mother was taking drugs for treatment of cystic acne. What was it?","o":["Vitamin D","Retinol acid","Vitamin C","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"a":1,"e":"Isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid, used for severe cystic acne) is a potent teratogen causing characteristic facial defects (cleft lip/palate, ear malformations), cardiac defects, and CNS anomalies in pregnancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D excess = hypercalcemia | Vitamin E excess = bleeding | Retinoid teratogen = isotretinoin (cystic acne drug)."},
/*132*/ {"q":"A 26-year-old female with a breast mass that is freely movable and non-tender, mammography normal. Next step in management?","o":["Mammogram","Ultrasound","FNAC","CT scan","MRI"],"a":1,"e":"In women <30 years, ultrasound is the first-line imaging for breast masses (denser breast tissue reduces mammography sensitivity); USG distinguishes solid vs cystic and characterises mass.<br><br>Take Notes: Mammography = >40 years | FNAC = after imaging | CT/MRI = staging | Young female + mass = ultrasound first."},
/*133*/ {"q":"Which one passes through greater sciatic foramen?","o":["Adductor longus","Adductor magnus","Tibial nerve","Piriformis","Sciatic nerve"],"a":3,"e":"Piriformis muscle passes through the greater sciatic foramen and divides it into suprapiriform and infrapiriform compartments. Sciatic nerve typically passes below piriformis (infrapiriform).<br><br>Take Notes: Sciatic nerve = below piriformis usually | Pudendal = lesser sciatic foramen | Greater sciatic foramen = piriformis."},
/*134*/ {"q":"Structure through greater sciatic notch:","o":["Piriformis","Superior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal vessel","Inferior gluteal nerve and vessel","Sciatic nerve"],"a":0,"e":"Piriformis is the principal landmark muscle passing through the greater sciatic foramen, dividing it into suprapiriform and infrapiriform compartments through which other vessels and nerves pass.<br><br>Take Notes: Superior gluteal vessels/nerve = suprapiriform | Sciatic/pudendal = infrapiriform | Greater sciatic notch = piriformis."},
/*135*/ {"q":"Which of the following marker is raised in case of an adnexal mass?","o":["AFP","LDH","CEA","CA 125","β-hCG"],"a":3,"e":"CA-125 is the primary tumour marker for epithelial ovarian cancer and adnexal masses, raised in >80% of serous ovarian carcinomas. Also elevated in endometriosis, PID, pregnancy.<br><br>Take Notes: AFP = yolk sac tumour, HCC | LDH = dysgerminoma | CEA = colon | Adnexal mass = CA 125."},
/*136*/ {"q":"Which of the following muscles would escape paralysis in a case of anterior mandibular nerve damage?","o":["Mylohyoid","Anterior belly of digastric","Omohyoid","Tensor tympani","Medial pterygoid"],"a":2,"e":"Anterior division of mandibular nerve (V3) supplies muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini. Omohyoid is supplied by ansa cervicalis (C1-C3), so it escapes.<br><br>Take Notes: Digastric (anterior)/mylohyoid = V3 | Medial pterygoid = V3 | Omohyoid = ansa cervicalis = escapes."},
/*137*/ {"q":"A person picks up different flowers and perceives various smells. Which process is responsible for distinguishing these different scents?","o":["Modulation","Integration","Adaptation","Discrimination","Transduction"],"a":3,"e":"Sensory discrimination distinguishes between different stimuli (e.g., different odours). Olfactory discrimination uses diverse receptor specificities and cortical processing in the orbitofrontal cortex.<br><br>Take Notes: Modulation = signal strength | Adaptation = decreased response | Discrimination = distinguishing different stimuli."},
/*138*/ {"q":"Which of the following are cardiac effects of digitalis?","o":["Dromotropy (decreased)","Inotropy (increased)","Chronotropy (decreased)","All of the above","None"],"a":3,"e":"Digoxin has multiple cardiac effects: positive inotropy (Na/K pump inhibition increases intracellular Ca), negative dromotropy (slowed AV conduction), negative chronotropy (vagal effect on SA node).<br><br>Take Notes: All three effects occur = comprehensive cardiac action | Increased contractility + slowed conduction + slowed rate = digoxin."},
/*139*/ {"q":"Demarcation of Upper GIT from Lower GIT is shown by:","o":["Lower GIT","Ligament of Treitz","Superior mesenteric artery","Falciform ligament","Tail of pancreas"],"a":1,"e":"Ligament of Treitz (suspensory ligament of duodenum) at duodenojejunal flexure divides foregut/upper GIT (proximal) from midgut/lower GIT (distal), important for localising GI bleeds (upper vs lower).<br><br>Take Notes: SMA = midgut blood supply | Falciform = liver | Upper/Lower GIT = Ligament of Treitz."},
/*140*/ {"q":"Biceps femoris nerve supply is shown by:","o":["Tibial & Common Peroneal (CPN)","Sciatic","Tibial only","Common Peroneal only","Femoral"],"a":0,"e":"Biceps femoris has dual innervation: long head by tibial division of sciatic nerve; short head by common peroneal division. This unique dual supply distinguishes it from other hamstrings.<br><br>Take Notes: Other hamstrings = tibial division only | Short head biceps femoris = common peroneal | Biceps femoris = both tibial + CPN."},
/*141*/ {"q":"Patient after RTA there is injury above the sacral segment of the spinal cord has which of the following effect?","o":["Bladder injury","Atonic Bladder","Autonomous bladder","Automatic Bladder","Neurogenic Bladder"],"a":3,"e":"Suprasacral spinal cord injury (above S2-S4) interrupts descending control but preserves sacral reflex arc; bladder becomes \"automatic\" (reflex/spastic) - emptying via intact sacral reflex with limited control.<br><br>Take Notes: Sacral injury = atonic (autonomous) bladder | Suprasacral = automatic (reflex) | Above sacral = automatic bladder."},
/*142*/ {"q":"What forms the roof of lateral ventricles?","o":["Spastic and atonic bladder","Upper part of corpus callosum","Body of corpus callosum","Cerebral Cortex","Genu of corpus callosum"],"a":2,"e":"The body of corpus callosum forms the roof of the body of lateral ventricles. The genu forms the anterior roof of frontal horns; splenium forms the roof of trigone area.<br><br>Take Notes: Genu = anterior roof (frontal horn) | Splenium = posterior | Body of lateral ventricle roof = body of corpus callosum."},
/*143*/ {"q":"Atherosclerosis plaque is formed of which of the following?","o":["Smooth muscle, C, T neutrophil, smooth muscle lymphocyte","Smooth muscle, Lymphocytes, intracellular Lipids","Smooth Muscle Cells, Macrophages and T cell + Extracellular Matrix","Extracellular Matrix Components","Collagen Elastic Fibers"],"a":2,"e":"Atherosclerotic plaque has 3 main components: cells (smooth muscle, macrophages/foam cells, T lymphocytes), extracellular matrix (collagen, elastic fibers, proteoglycans), and lipids (intra/extracellular).<br><br>Take Notes: Foam cells = lipid-laden macrophages | Robbins triad = cells + ECM + lipid | Atherosclerosis = SMC + macrophages + T cells + ECM."},
/*144*/ {"q":"IVC blocked up to azygous vein. Dilation will be in lumbar and right:","o":["Right ascending lumbar vein","Subcostal vein","Ascending vein","Azygous vein","Hemiazygous vein"],"a":1,"e":"IVC obstruction below azygos junction → collateral flow via ascending lumbar veins to azygos system. The subcostal vein (12th intercostal level) joins right ascending lumbar vein, contributing to azygos.<br><br>Take Notes: Azygos drains to SVC = bypass route | Lumbar → azygos system | IVC block + azygos collateral = subcostal vein."},
/*145*/ {"q":"A 45-year-old office worker presents with numbness, tingling, and pain in the first three fingers of her right hand, worse at night. Weakness in grip and frequent dropping of objects. Thenar muscle atrophy on examination. Most commonly used diagnostic test?","o":["Phalen's test","Tinel's sign","Finkelstein's test","Allen's test","Adson's test"],"a":0,"e":"Carpal tunnel syndrome (median nerve compression at wrist): Phalen's test (90° wrist flexion for 60 seconds reproducing symptoms) is the most commonly used clinical test, complemented by Tinel's sign.<br><br>Take Notes: Tinel's = percussion test | Finkelstein's = de Quervain | Allen's = vascular | Carpal tunnel = Phalen's test."},
/*146*/ {"q":"Female patient had aortic root dilation up to 4 cm, ocular examination showed lens dislocation, mitral valve leaflet defect with systolic click. Defect in which of the following?","o":["Spectrin","Dystrophin","Laminin","Fibrillin","Collagen"],"a":3,"e":"Marfan syndrome: autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation causes fibrillin-1 defect; clinical triad of aortic root dilation, ectopia lentis (upward lens dislocation), and skeletal/MVP findings. Risk of aortic dissection.<br><br>Take Notes: Dystrophin = Duchenne | Spectrin = hereditary spherocytosis | Marfan = fibrillin-1 (FBN1)."},
/*147*/ {"q":"Most common complication of rubella in 2 months pregnant lady:","o":["Cataract","Deafness","PDA","Microcephaly","Cardiac defect"],"a":0,"e":"Congenital rubella syndrome timing: first 7 weeks = cataracts predominate; weeks 7-12 = deafness; after 12 weeks = milder defects. At 2 months (early), cataracts are most common complication.<br><br>Take Notes: <7 weeks = cataracts | 7-12 weeks = deafness | Overall most common = deafness | At 2 months = cataracts."},
/*148*/ {"q":"Child with hepatosplenomegaly, streaks on hair and face, abdomen protruded. Cause:","o":["Kwashiorkor","Marasmus","Physical abuse","CLD","Cystic fibrosis"],"a":0,"e":"Kwashiorkor: severe protein malnutrition with adequate calories — hepatomegaly (fatty liver), oedema, hair changes (flag sign with depigmented streaks), abdominal distension. Distinct from marasmus (caloric deficit).<br><br>Take Notes: Marasmus = total caloric deficit, wasted | Kwashiorkor = protein deficit, oedematous | Streaks on hair + protruded abdomen = kwashiorkor."},
/*149*/ {"q":"Type of necrosis in brain:","o":["Coagulative","Liquefactive","Caseous","Fibrinoid","Fat"],"a":1,"e":"Brain undergoes liquefactive necrosis after ischaemic injury due to high lipid content and absence of robust supporting stroma; microglial enzymes digest tissue rapidly, leaving cystic cavities.<br><br>Take Notes: Coagulative = heart, kidney, spleen | Caseous = TB | Liquefactive = brain + bacterial abscess."},
/*150*/ {"q":"Patient presented with paroxysmal fever, chills and time duration for symptoms appearing was 36-48 hours. Ring and crescent shaped organism involved likely is:","o":["P. vivax","P. knowlesi","P. falciparum","P. malariae","Dengue"],"a":2,"e":"Plasmodium falciparum has 36-48 hour periodicity and shows ring and crescent (banana-shaped gametocytes) forms on smear. P. falciparum causes the most severe malaria with cerebral and renal complications.<br><br>Take Notes: P. vivax/ovale = 48h (tertian) | P. malariae = 72h (quartan) | Crescent gametocytes = P. falciparum."},
/*151*/ {"q":"CO2 transports mainly in the form of:","o":["HCO3","Free form","Bound to chloride","Dissolved in plasma","Bound to Hb"],"a":0,"e":"CO2 is transported in blood mainly (70%) as bicarbonate (HCO3-) via carbonic anhydrase in RBCs. About 23% binds Hb as carbamino compounds; only 7% is dissolved in plasma.<br><br>Take Notes: Dissolved = 7% | Hb-bound = 23% | HCO3- = 70% (main form) of CO2 transport."},
/*152*/ {"q":"A postmortem clot is most likely found to be resembling:","o":["Chicken fat","Butter","Cheese","Jelly","Glue"],"a":0,"e":"Postmortem clots have a characteristic \"chicken fat\" appearance (yellow upper layer from settled plasma) over \"red currant jelly\" (lower red layer with settled RBCs), with smooth shiny surface and unattached.<br><br>Take Notes: Antemortem clot = dry, friable, attached to wall | Postmortem = chicken fat + currant jelly = unattached."},
/*153*/ {"q":"Patient with anterior 2/3 tongue carcinoma. What is the lymphatic drainage?","o":["Jugulo-omohyoid","Digastric","Submandibular","Submental","Deep cervical"],"a":2,"e":"Anterior 2/3 of tongue drains primarily to submandibular lymph nodes (then to deep cervical chain). Tip drains to submental; posterior 1/3 drains to jugulo-omohyoid nodes directly.<br><br>Take Notes: Tip of tongue = submental | Posterior 1/3 = jugulo-omohyoid | Anterior 2/3 = submandibular nodes."},
/*154*/ {"q":"Which part of the colon is most commonly affected by Hirschsprung disease?","o":["Ascending colon and transverse colon","Sigmoid colon","Cecum and ileum","Transverse colon and descending colon","Rectum"],"a":1,"e":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) most commonly affects the rectosigmoid region (75-80%), where absence of ganglion cells in myenteric and submucosal plexuses causes functional obstruction.<br><br>Take Notes: Short-segment = rectosigmoid (most common) | Long-segment = up to splenic flexure | Hirschsprung = sigmoid colon (rectosigmoid)."},
/*155*/ {"q":"A section of caudal pons consists of horizontal fibers. These fibers most likely consist of:","o":["Superior cerebellar peduncle","Middle cerebellar peduncle","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Subthalamus","Thalamus"],"a":1,"e":"Middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP) consists of horizontal pontocerebellar fibers entering the cerebellum from the contralateral pontine nuclei, forming the bulk of the pons in caudal sections.<br><br>Take Notes: Superior cerebellar peduncle = midbrain connection | Inferior = medulla connection | Caudal pons horizontal fibers = middle cerebellar peduncle."},
/*156*/ {"q":"A patient presents with chest pain that radiates to the back and is not related to respiration. On examination, muffled heart sounds are noted along the left sternal border. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Pericarditis","Myocarditis","Cardiac tamponade","Costochondritis","Aortic dissection"],"a":2,"e":"Beck's triad (muffled heart sounds, hypotension, raised JVP) indicates cardiac tamponade. Chest pain radiating to back unrelated to respiration with muffled heart sounds is classic for tamponade.<br><br>Take Notes: Pericarditis = pleuritic, friction rub | Costochondritis = chest wall tender | Muffled sounds + radiation to back = cardiac tamponade."},
/*157*/ {"q":"During hysterectomy and ovarian surgery, the ureter is most at risk where it:","o":["Crosses the pelvic brim at the common iliac","Lies near the internal iliac artery","Crosses beneath the uterine artery (water under the bridge)","Enters the bladder trigone","Passes through the broad ligament"],"a":2,"e":"The ureter is at greatest risk where the uterine artery crosses over it (\"water under the bridge\") in the cardinal ligament, about 2 cm lateral to the cervix.<br><br>Take Notes: Pelvic brim crossing = also vulnerable | Bladder trigone entry = late danger | Uterine artery overlying ureter = surgical danger."},
/*158*/ {"q":"Patient with chronic diarrhoea presents with bruising and bleeding gums. PT and APTT prolonged, bleeding time normal, no family history. Most likely diagnosis:","o":["Hemophilia A","Classical hemophilia","Liver failure","Vitamin K deficiency","Von Willebrand disease"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic diarrhoea causes fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption. Vitamin K is needed for factors II, VII, IX, X, so deficiency prolongs PT and APTT but spares bleeding time and platelet function.<br><br>Take Notes: Christmas disease = Factor IX, X-linked, family history | Vitamin K = factors II, VII, IX, X = prolonged PT + APTT."},
];
